h moderates", "Democrats have a deep bench, with Mark Kelly and Ruben Gallego drawing national attention as strong potential nominees", "Open race with no incumbent on the ballot removes any incumbency cushion for either side"], "rationale": "With no polls, expert ratings, or market data available, the forecast rests on Arizona's two-point Republican lean, with a slight nudge toward Democrats from headlines showing polarizing, election-denier-linked GOP primary winners and an unusually strong Democratic bench. That nudge is not enough to overcome the fundamentals, leaving a genuine tossup with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "The first credible general-election polls or expert ratings, and whether the eventual nominees moderate or emphasize election denial, could move this forecast quickly in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arizona sits about two points to the right of the country, and with no incumbent on the ballot, neither side starts with an incumbency edge. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's partisan fundamentals, which point to a narrow Republican edge and a genuinely open contest.\n\nThe candidate landscape looks volatile. Republican primaries favored Trump-aligned contenders, including figures associated with efforts to overturn the 2020 election, while Democrats field a deep bench led by figures like Mark Kelly and Ruben Gallego. Polarizing nominees have cost Republicans in Arizona before, but presidential races tend to revert to the state's underlying lean. Expect a close race shaped by turnout and candidate contrast.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4092, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4294, "margin":